1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,956
Orchard Core keeps the ticket of a signed-in user in a cookie.

2
00:00:04,956 --> 00:00:09,784
The Users Authentication Ticket Store feature keeps it on the server instead.

3
00:00:09,784 --> 00:00:13,623
In this video, we'll see why, how to enable it, how its code

4
00:00:13,623 --> 00:00:15,950
works, and where the tickets live.

5
00:00:20,550 --> 00:00:25,173
By default, the application cookie holds the whole authentication ticket.

6
00:00:25,173 --> 00:00:29,797
With the Roles module enabled, the ticket carries the claims of the user,

7
00:00:29,797 --> 00:00:34,420
including one for each role and, unless the user is an administrator, one

8
00:00:34,420 --> 00:00:36,820
for each permission of those roles.

9
00:00:37,570 --> 00:00:41,690
The more roles and permissions a user has, the larger the cookie.

10
00:00:41,690 --> 00:00:48,130
The documentation describes requests that fail when the cookie pushes the request headers over the limit.

11
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:52,835
With the ticket store, the ticket stays on the server, in a cache.

12
00:00:52,835 --> 00:00:56,680
The browser keeps a cookie that holds only a session identifier.

13
00:01:00,898 --> 00:01:04,858
You enable the ticket store on the Features page, under Tools.

14
00:01:04,858 --> 00:01:10,738
Search for ticket, and the Users Authentication Ticket Store appears, in the Security category.

15
00:01:11,202 --> 00:01:16,650
The row shows the name, the identifier that recipes use, and the description.

16
00:01:16,650 --> 00:01:21,112
The Users badge is its dependency, and its status is Disabled.

17
00:01:21,568 --> 00:01:23,840
To turn it on, you select Enable.

18
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:27,858
The feature has no settings page and no permissions of its own.

19
00:01:29,458 --> 00:01:33,617
In a recipe, the Feature step enables a feature by its identifier.

20
00:01:33,617 --> 00:01:38,988
List the ticket store there, and any site that runs the recipe has the feature enabled.

21
00:01:43,188 --> 00:01:46,829
The feature has one startup class, tied to its identifier.

22
00:01:46,829 --> 00:01:51,549
It registers the configuration of the cookie options, so nothing changes in a

23
00:01:51,549 --> 00:01:53,938
tenant until the feature is enabled.

24
00:01:54,688 --> 00:02:00,738
That configuration sets the SessionStore of the Identity application cookie, and of no other cookie.

25
00:02:00,738 --> 00:02:06,160
The store is a CacheTicketStore, an ITicketStore built from the IDistributedCache and the

26
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:08,728
Data Protection provider of the tenant.

27
00:02:09,478 --> 00:02:14,535
StoreAsync serializes the ticket, protects it with Data Protection, and writes it to

28
00:02:14,535 --> 00:02:16,502
the cache under a unique key.

29
00:02:16,502 --> 00:02:20,828
The entry expires when the ticket does, and the method returns the key.

30
00:02:21,578 --> 00:02:27,354
RetrieveAsync reads the entry by its key, unprotects it, and deserializes the ticket.

31
00:02:27,354 --> 00:02:30,781
When the entry is missing, it returns no ticket.

32
00:02:30,781 --> 00:02:36,048
When the entry can't be unprotected, it logs the error and returns no ticket.

33
00:02:36,798 --> 00:02:39,135
RemoveAsync deletes the entry.

34
00:02:39,135 --> 00:02:45,494
RenewAsync writes the ticket again with its expiration, and logs a failure without throwing.

35
00:02:45,494 --> 00:02:49,258
StoreAsync also logs a failure, but throws it again.

36
00:02:53,458 --> 00:02:57,525
The store uses whichever IDistributedCache the tenant registers.

37
00:02:57,525 --> 00:03:01,534
By default, that is a memory cache, one for each tenant, so the

38
00:03:01,534 --> 00:03:06,298
tickets live in memory, and a restart or a reload of the tenant clears them.

39
00:03:07,048 --> 00:03:12,043
With several instances, enable Redis Cache and set the Redis configuration, and the

40
00:03:12,043 --> 00:03:16,084
tickets go to Redis under the instance prefix and the tenant name.

41
00:03:16,084 --> 00:03:20,798
If the configuration is missing or invalid, the tenant keeps the memory cache.

42
00:03:21,548 --> 00:03:26,819
Because tickets are protected with Data Protection, instances that share a cache must

43
00:03:26,819 --> 00:03:28,382
share their keys too.

44
00:03:28,382 --> 00:03:35,158
Distributed Data Protection (Redis) and Distributed Data Protection (Azure Storage) keep the keys in one place.

45
00:03:37,158 --> 00:03:39,852
That's the Users Authentication Ticket Store:

46
00:03:39,852 --> 00:03:43,391
it keeps the ticket on the server, in memory or in Redis, and

47
00:03:43,391 --> 00:03:46,085
leaves the browser with a session identifier.

48
00:03:46,085 --> 00:03:47,458
Thanks for watching.

